{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792489465,"nonce":"SCXUafIgPbJ5VC39iqgs/dVeLo4=","title":"The JIT keeps tens of MB of compiler structures after tiering: LLVM debug-info metadata and cranelift data stay live","message":"A large Haxe app (RayDE, on ashui) that opens 14 files in tabs and closes them all holds about 25 MB more native memory afterwards than a fresh window, and the growth is the JIT's, not the program's. Closing the tabs frees everything the program owns; these blocks are still live after a major collection.\n\n**How it was measured**\n\nRun the program with MallocStackLogging=1, take `malloc_history \u003cpid\u003e -callTree -collapseRecursion` after startup and again after the tabs are closed and the collector has run, and diff the live bytes by the nearest three frames outside the allocator. The scratch driver is /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-amaterasu-Vibranium-ashui/ab292b42-0b79-454c-aa0c-2c901f96c1a1/scratchpad/rmem/src/TabsMemory.hx (CLOSEALL=1), and the diff script is rmem/mhdiff.py next to it. The blinc_abi library in that run was a debug build; its own growth was about 6 MB.\n\n**What grew (live MB, from start to after close)**\n\n- ash_core::cranelift_jit and beadie_cranelift: +4.8\n- llvm::DILocation::getImpl, 74,734 live blocks: +4.6\n- ash_core::air_pipeline::optimized_with_config, Function::shrink_to_fit: +3.7 and +0.8\n- ash_core::jit_map::set_positions: +2.8\n- llvm::raw_svector_ostream::write_impl: +2.7\n- llvm::sys::Memory::allocateMappedMemory and SectionMemoryManager: +1.4\n- ash_core::llvm::lines::attach_loaded: +1.4\n\nTogether about 25 MB. Also, hlp_hiset (Haxe int hash tables) grew by 22,000 blocks and 4.6 MB and hlp_hi64alloc by 25,777 blocks and 2.4 MB; those may be live program maps, not the JIT.\n\n**Question**\n\nIs the LLVM debug-info metadata meant to outlive the compile of each function, and can the air and cranelift per-function data be dropped once the code is installed? If it is the line table for backtraces, a compact form would do.","files":null}]}